"""Unicode recovery for Mangal subsets whose ``GSUB`` table was stripped.

Mangal is the Devanagari face Microsoft ships with Windows, and it is what most
of the OAG corpus's born-digital Nepali is set in once the legacy keystroke fonts
are excluded. The subsets embedded here are ``/Identity-H`` ``CIDFontType2`` with
a ``ToUnicode`` CMap that is wrong in two systematic ways -- ``कार्यालय`` comes back
as ``कायालय`` (the repha renamed) and the pre-base ``ि`` comes back as whatever
consonant its width variant happens to sit over -- and with ``GSUB`` removed, so
:func:`likhit.extractors.kalimati._get_font_correction_map`'s reconstruction sees
only the font's own ``cmap``: enough for the base letters, nothing for the
conjuncts, the half-forms, the repha or the matra variants, which is exactly the
damaged class.

This module is Mangal's half of what
:mod:`likhit.extractors.kalimati_reference` is for Kalimati, built the same way
and keyed the same way, and the two are read together by
``kalimati._get_font_correction_map``.

Keyed on outlines, not glyph ids
--------------------------------
Measured on this corpus, the Mangal embeds do NOT share one glyph order: the
subsets report 886, 885, 675, 195 and 2 glyphs, at two units-per-em values, and
the same drawing appears at different ids in different subsets -- ``ल`` was found
at id 155 in one subset and at 871 in another. A CID-keyed table would therefore
decode part of the cohort confidently and wrongly, which is the one failure this
corpus cannot absorb. An outline identifies a *drawing*, so it is order- and
name-independent, and it needs no font-identity guard: a font that is not this
design does not match, because matching means identical contours coordinate for
coordinate.

Provenance
----------
The table is not hand-written and it is not derived from an external font. It is
what ``kalimati._get_font_correction_map``'s own derivation produces -- ``cmap``
reversal, :func:`~likhit.extractors.kalimati._infer_mark_variants`, then
:func:`~likhit.extractors.kalimati._analyze_gsub` -- run over every Mangal embed
in the corpus that still carries ``GSUB``, re-keyed from glyph id to outline
digest, and merged. Using many subsets rather than one reference font is what
makes the table larger than any single embed could support: the most complete
single embed derives 476 mappings, the union derives 1137.

Corroboration
-------------
A wrong entry is worse than a missing one. A missing one leaves the glyph with
whatever the PDF said; a wrong one silently substitutes a consonant, and this
project has measured a vision-based read doing that at 71.4% with no
``U+FFFD`` and no ratio drop to give it away. So an entry ships only with
evidence from outside its own derivation. Four signals, each independent of the
derivation and of each other:

``cmap``
    the value came from the font's own Unicode ``cmap``, a different table from
    the ``GSUB`` the conjuncts come from.
``tounicode``
    some document's *authored* ``ToUnicode`` CMap gives the same value for the
    same outline. These CMaps are the broken thing, so this is positive evidence
    only -- a disagreement is the damage, not a veto. Counted per DOCUMENT.
``kalimati``
    :data:`likhit.extractors.kalimati_reference.OUTLINE_TO_UNICODE` -- a
    different font, a different investigation, already reviewed -- gives the
    same value at the same glyph id. Kalimati is a Mangal-derived face and the
    two orders coincide over most of their range: measured agreement is
    99.4% across 851 embeds, so a disagreement is worth
    inspecting rather than dismissing.
``words``
    decoding the page's own glyph stream through the entry assembles known OAG
    vocabulary. This is a second instrument, not a second derivation: the glyph
    order on the page is evidence the font tables do not contain.

The bar, applied by `_recon/scripts/emit_module.py::ships` at generation time:

* at least **2** independent font programs derive the entry, and
* at least **one** of the four signals speaks for it, and
* a ``kalimati`` contradiction is a veto unless ``words`` supports the Mangal
  reading.

Of 1137 candidate outlines, **961** clear the bar and 176 do not. Signal coverage over the shipped entries: cmap 209, tounicode 477, kalimati 930, words 276.

Entries that fail the bar are recorded in
:data:`UNCORROBORATED_OUTLINE_TO_UNICODE`, which nothing reads. They are kept so
the next investigation does not re-derive them, and because a table that silently
drops its residue cannot be audited.

Three readings of ra, told apart by geometry
--------------------------------------------
``_analyze_gsub`` gives every ``rphf`` output the repha value ``र्``, and this
font reaches its below-form ra and its in-line half-form the same way, so the
value alone cannot separate three different glyphs. Geometry can, and the test is
the one :mod:`likhit.extractors.kalimati_reference` states for its CID 89:

=========================== ================= ==================================
 measured                    reading           handling
=========================== ================= ==================================
 above the headline, adv 0   repha             gets the reordering marker
 below the baseline, adv 0   below-form (rakar) value is ``्र``; no marker
 in the line body, adv > 0   in-line half-form value is ``र्``; NO marker
=========================== ================= ==================================

Getting the third one wrong turns ``प्र`` into ``र्प`` -- not a hole but a
different word. :data:`IN_LINE_RA_DIGESTS` records the outlines exempt from the
marker and :func:`mangal_in_line_ra_cids` is how a caller applies the exemption.

There is deliberately no ``BELOW_FORM_RA_CORRECTIONS`` here, unlike
:mod:`likhit.extractors.lohit` and :mod:`likhit.extractors.kalimati_reference`.
Mangal builds its below-form ra with a ``blwf`` SINGLE substitution, which
``_analyze_gsub`` already reads as ``virama + ra``; Lohit builds the same glyph
with a ligature rule, which it reads as ``ra + virama`` and has to swap back. The
one Mangal outline where subsets disagree -- some reach it through ``rphf`` and
value it ``र्`` -- is resolved to ``्र`` in the table itself and recorded in
:data:`RESOLVED_CONFLICTS`; measured, its geometry puts it entirely below the
baseline, and the ``र्`` reading duplicates a value the same subsets already
assign to a different glyph.

Values are plain Unicode. Turning the visual-order marks into reordering markers
is the caller's job -- see
:func:`likhit.extractors.lohit.with_reordering_markers` -- so this module stays a
faithful record of what the fonts say.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from collections.abc import Container
from typing import Any

from likhit.extractors.kalimati_reference import outline_digest

